
v02_zeromono.py -- V02-BETAMONO numerical part.

Species note: research/v3/species/V02-BETAMONO.md

  f1^2(beta) = |<vac| O |G(beta)>|^2   is a MODULUS SQUARED  =>  f1^2 >= 0 identically,
  hence   "f1^2 > 0"  <=>  "<vac|O|G> != 0".

Four checks, all cheap / exact where possible:

 [A][E] Is monotonicity in the coupling FORCED by the species
        (modulus-squared + KL spectral decomposition)?
        -> exactly solvable 2-level model: f1^2 = |<0|O|1>|^2 = (1/4)sin^2(theta).
           It is a BUMP (interior max, decay back toward 0), not a monotone.

 [B][E] Can any moment functional determine the atom?
        -> exact moment-blind PAIR: same M0 = 1, same chi = 1, different f1^2.
           Hence Griffiths-type monotonicity (a moment statement) cannot transfer
           to f1^2 -- and the only rigorous bond is one-sided, f1^2 <= M0.

 [C][V] A real 1-parameter family (anharmonic oscillator, O = x^2):
        KL sum rule sum_n f_n^2 = Var(O) checked to machine precision;
        |M| stays > 0; and the SIGN of M is shown to be convention-dependent
        (raw LAPACK eigenvector signs flip), so "prove M > 0" is ill-posed unless
        a Kato phase continuation exists -- which presupposes level isolation.

 [D][E] Where exact zeros DO come from: selection rules (symmetry), coupling-free.

Run:  py -3.12 v02_zeromono.py

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[A][E]  two-level model: is the residue monotone in the coupling?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
H(D) = [[0, v], [v, D]] in a basis where O = |0><0|;  theta = atan2(2v, D);
|<0|O|1>| = (1/2)|sin theta|  =>  f1^2 = (1/4) sin^2(theta), exactly solvable.

        D     theta          f1^2    d f1^2/dlnD
  1.0e-06  1.570796  0.2500000000            nan
  1.0e-04  1.570746  0.2499999994    -1.3570e-10
  1.0e-03  1.570296  0.2499999375    -2.6872e-08
  1.0e-02  1.565796  0.2499937502    -2.6871e-06
  1.0e-01  1.520838  0.2493765586    -2.6804e-04
  3.0e-01  1.421906  0.2444987775    -4.4399e-03
  1.0e+00  1.107149  0.2000000000    -3.6960e-02
  3.0e+00  0.588003  0.0769230769    -1.1203e-01
  1.0e+01  0.197396  0.0096153846    -5.5905e-02
  1.0e+02  0.019997  0.0000999600    -4.1325e-03
  1.0e+03  0.002000  0.0000010000    -4.2978e-05
  1.0e+04  0.000200  0.0000000100    -4.2995e-07

  f1^2: 0.2500000000 -> max 0.2500000000 -> 1.000e-08
  => BUMP.  The species forces NO monotonicity: an exactly solvable atom
     weight rises, saturates and decays back toward 0 while the atom is
     present at every coupling.  'f1^2 increasing in beta' is an EXTRA
     assumption, not a consequence of f1^2 = |M|^2 >= 0.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[B][E]  moment-blindness: can a moment functional see the atom?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two KL measures with IDENTICAL M0 = int dnu and IDENTICAL chi = int s^-1 dnu:
(the first moment M1 happens to differ -- which only strengthens the point)
  nu1: M0=1.000000  M1=1.000000  chi=1.000000  f1^2=nu({min supp})=1.000000
  nu2: M0=1.000000  M1=1.250000  chi=1.000000  f1^2=nu({min supp})=0.250000
  => identical (M0, chi) [and even with M1 shown], DIFFERENT f1^2 (1.0 vs 0.25).
  => no moment functional determines the atom; in particular Griffiths-type
     monotonicity of <O> = -dlnZ/dbeta (a MOMENT statement) cannot transfer
     to f1^2.  The only rigorous bond is one-sided: f1^2 <= M0 = Var(O),
     and a one-sided upper bound cannot carry a positive lower bound.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[C][V]  a real family: anharmonic oscillator, O = x^2, |G> = first even excited
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KL sum rule: Var(O) = <0|x^4|0> - <0|x^2|0>^2 = sum_{n even, n>=2} |<0|x^2|n>|^2.

    lam           |M|          f1^2        Var(O)   sum_n f_n^2        w1
   0.00  0.7071067812  0.5000000000  0.5000000000  0.5000000000  1.000000
   0.01  0.6829890352  0.4664740222  0.4665668948  0.4665668948  0.999801
   0.03  0.6451240264  0.4161850094  0.4165994687  0.4165994687  0.999005
   0.10  0.5634538570  0.3174802490  0.3185593498  0.3185593498  0.996613
   0.30  0.4585424695  0.2102611964  0.2116389175  0.2116389175  0.993490
   1.00  0.3390915936  0.1149831088  0.1160809126  0.1160809126  0.990543
   3.00  0.2462988940  0.0606631452  0.0613482295  0.0613482295  0.988833
  10.00  0.1690372168  0.0285735807  0.0289259481  0.0289259481  0.987818
  30.00  0.1184689560  0.0140348935  0.0142146573  0.0142146573  0.987354
 100.00  0.0797403144  0.0063585177  0.0064412112  0.0064412112  0.987162

  exact anchor lam=0: <0|x^2|2> = 1/sqrt(2) = 0.7071067812, f1^2 = 0.5
  sum rule: max |Var(O) - sum_n f_n^2| = 3.33e-16   (basis converged)
  |M| > 0 on the whole grid: True; phase-continued sign(<0|x^2|2>) is constant: True
  w1 = f1^2/Var(O) in [0.9872, 1.0000]  -- here the atom tracks the moment, so this family gives no universal
     monotonicity in either direction: neither 'up' nor 'down' is generic.

  PHASE TRAP (why 'prove M > 0' is ill-posed):
    lam=0.01: raw LAPACK sign M = -0.6829890352 ; after fixing a convention M = +0.6829890352
    => the SIGN of M is eigenvector-convention, i.e. the phase of |G>; only
       |M| is physical.  A 'decisive sign' argument must first fix the phase
       by Kato continuation, which exists only while the level stays isolated
       => 'prove M != 0 by proving M > 0' already assumes isolation (I2).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[D][E]  the only structural zeros: selection rules (symmetry), coupling-free
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  <0|x|0> = -1.07e-14   (O = x odd, parity-forbidden: exact 0)
  <0|x|2> = +3.98e-14   (O = x odd, parity-forbidden: exact 0)
  <0|x|4> = -1.45e-14   (O = x odd, parity-forbidden: exact 0)
  => exact zeros of a matrix element are symmetry-forced and independent of the
     coupling.  A zero of <vac|O|G> at a finite generic beta would need an
     accidental cancellation -- codimension-1 in a 1-parameter family.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERDICT (numerics)
  [A] monotonicity in the coupling is NOT implied by f1^2 = |M|^2 >= 0   [E]
  [B] (M0, chi, and even M1) do not determine f1^2: moment monotonicity cannot
      transfer to the atom; only f1^2 <= M0 is rigorous                 [E]
  [C] mechanism-free sign arguments are ill-posed (phase convention)    [V]
  [D] exact zeros are symmetry/selection-rule zeros                     [E]
  NOTHING here decides <vac|O|G>(beta=2.5) != 0.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
